IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hrpa-dates`

-- Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) -- the questions of the consumer: the person whose health record
-- it is, who wants to see it, have it explained, copied, sent on, corrected or kept, or who is asked to consent to a
-- report about their health.
--
-- SEAT: the consumer (dictionary: "an individual who uses, or has used, a health service, or in relation to whom a
-- health record has been created"), and the people the Act lets act for one: a person with parental responsibility for
-- a child or young person, a guardian of a legally incompetent person, the legal representative (or, if none, an
-- immediate family member) of a consumer who has died. Typically the patient getting their records before a medical
-- negligence or injury claim, or the injured person whose insurer or employer wants a medical report. "Can I see my
-- record?", "By when must I be answered?", "Is the record keeper taken to have refused me?", "Can they get a report
-- about me without my consent?", "When may my record be destroyed?". The record keeper, the health service provider,
-- the health services commissioner, the human rights commission, the director-general and the courts get no seat: what
-- they must do is encoded as what the consumer must be given and by when, and what they believe or are satisfied of is
-- asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
-- The duties with deadlines the consumer meets (s 13 (2) answer within 2 weeks; s 13B / s 13BA give access by the
-- 30-day or fee day; s 16B give the record to the nominated provider within 2 weeks; principles 12.1 and 12.2 send the
-- record to the new provider) are regulative rules run by EVALTRACE inside the questions.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 7 (health status reports); Part 3 access (ss 9, 10 (1)-(3), (7), (8), 10A, 12, 13 (with
-- (5)), 13A, 13B, 13BA (2), (6), 13C, 13D, 14, 14A, 15, 16A, 16B, 16D, 17; not s 8, s 10 (4)-(6), s 11, s 13BA (3)-(5),
-- s 16 or s 16C); s 18 (complaint grounds); ss 20-23 (maximum penalties only); ss 25-27 (who acts for the consumer,
-- and the void consents of s 26 (2) and s 27 (3)); ss 31, 32 (court); schedule 1 principles 4.1 (keeping
-- and destruction), 6 cl 4 (being required to get one's own record), 7 cl 3 (statement on a refused correction), 10
-- cl 2 (c) and cl 7 (consent to disclosure, with s 13A), 11 (practice relocation or closure), 12.1 and 12.2 (transfer to
-- a new provider). Out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997, A1997-125, Republication No 32 (R32), effective 16 November
-- 2025, deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A1997-125.txt. The fees determined under s 34 are not deposited:
-- whether a fee is payable is asked.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Beliefs and satisfactions the Act hands to the record keeper, the
-- nominated provider or the court are INPUTS ("if the record keeper believes on reasonable grounds ..."). Answers follow
-- the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the
-- text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is NOTHING or marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997: the questions of the consumer`

-- The parties to the regulative rules, and the steps.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `the record keeper`
    `the first provider`

DECLARE `Step` IS ONE OF
    `answer the request for access`
    `give access to the record`
    `give the record to the nominated provider`
    `give the copy or summary to the new provider`

-- s 13 (2): "Within 2 weeks after the day the record keeper receives the request, the record keeper must -- ..." one of
-- the answers in (a)-(d). The days allowed are 14, or more under an agreement under s 9.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 13 (2) -- answer the request within 2 weeks` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the record keeper`
    MUST `answer the request for access`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the record keeper` BECAUSE "no answer to the request for access within 2 weeks after the day it was received (s 13 (2)); access is taken to have been refused (s 13D (a) (i))"

-- s 13B (2), s 13BA (2): access "must be given" no later than 30 days after the day the request is received, or, where a
-- fee notice was given and the fee paid, the later of 1 week after the payment and 30 days after the request. The days
-- allowed are counted from the day the request was received. A breach of s 13B is a deemed refusal (s 13D (a) (iii));
-- s 13D does not list s 13BA (finding 11).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 13B (2) -- give access by the last day` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the record keeper`
    MUST `give access to the record`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the record keeper` BECAUSE "access not given by the day s 13B (2) or s 13BA (2) sets (for s 13B, access is taken to have been refused: s 13D (a) (iii))"

-- s 16B (2): "The record keeper must give the health record, or a copy of it, to the nominated health service provider
-- within 2 weeks after the day the record keeper receives the nomination."
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 16B (2) -- give the record to the nominated provider within 2 weeks` MEANS
    PARTY `the record keeper`
    MUST `give the record to the nominated provider`
    WITHIN 14
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the record keeper` BECAUSE "the record was not given to the nominated health service provider within 2 weeks after the nomination was received (s 16B (2))"

-- Principles 12.1 cl 2 (b), 3, 4 and 12.2 cl 2 (b), 3, 4: the first provider (or first practice) must give the new
-- provider the copy or written summary within the days allowed, counted from the day the transfer request is received.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`principle 12 -- send the record to the new provider in time` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the first provider`
    MUST `give the copy or summary to the new provider`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the first provider` BECAUSE "the copy or written summary was not given to the new provider by the day principle 12.1 or 12.2 sets"

-- Runs a rule over one event on a day (counted from day 0). FULFILLED means in time. Not exported; takes a DEONTIC only
-- internally.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`, `the party` IS A `Party`, `the step` IS A `Step`, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `in time under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the party` `the step` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Timing` IS ONE OF
    `done in time`
    `not yet done, still in time`
    `out of time`

-- Whether a step taken (or, if not yet taken, still to be taken) is in time. A step taken before the period starts is
-- counted as taken on day 0.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`, `the party` IS A `Party`, `the step` IS A `Step`,
      `done` IS A BOOLEAN, `done on` IS A DATE, `today` IS A DATE, `the start` IS A DATE, `the deadline` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Timing`
`the timing under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `done` `done on` `today` `the start` `the deadline` MEANS
    IF `done`
    THEN IF `in time under` `the rule` `the party` `the step` `the day done`
         THEN `done in time`
         ELSE `out of time`
    ELSE IF `the deadline` `falls before` `today`
         THEN `out of time`
         ELSE `not yet done, still in time`
    WHERE
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `the start` `to` `done on`
        `the day done` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`

-- A deadline answer: a last day, or no deadline yet, or no duty.
DECLARE `Deadline` IS ONE OF
    `due by` HAS `the day it is due` IS A DATE
    `no deadline yet: the fee stated in the notice has not been paid`
    `no duty on these facts (the text's no)`

-- s 9: the parties may agree for an act to be done, or a notice given, "in another way or within a longer period"; done
-- in accordance with the agreement, it is taken to be done within the time. Only a later day counts.
GIVEN `the day the Act sets` IS A DATE, `we agreed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN, `the agreed last day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`with any s 9 agreement` `the day the Act sets` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day` MEANS
    IF `we agreed a longer period`
    THEN `the later of` `the day the Act sets` `and` `the agreed last day`
    ELSE `the day the Act sets`

-- The date of commencement of this Act: 1 February 1998 (endnote 3, legislation history: "commenced 1 February 1998
-- (s 2)"). Used by s 10 (1) (d) (and named in s 11 (2) (a) and s 13C (2) (b) (iii)).
`the date of commencement of this Act` MEANS `the calendar date` 1 2 1998

-- Who the consumer is, for the questions about who acts. Dictionary: "child" means a person under 12; "young person" is
-- borrowed from the Children and Young People Act 2008 s 12 (12 or older but not yet an adult; not deposited);
-- "legally incompetent person" means a person subject to an operative enduring power of attorney, or (otherwise than as
-- a person under the age of majority) to a guardianship order; "legal representative" of a deceased person means an
-- executor with probate granted or resealed in Australia, or an administrator of the estate in Australia.
DECLARE `The consumer is` IS ONE OF
    `an adult who is not a legally incompetent person`
    `a legally incompetent person`
    `a child (under 12)`
    `a young person with sufficient maturity and developmental capacity to understand the request and the record`
    `a young person without that maturity and capacity`
    `dead, and there is a legal representative`
    `dead, and there is no legal representative`

DECLARE `Who acts` IS ONE OF
    `the consumer personally`
    `a guardian of the consumer`
    `the person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    `the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    `an immediate family member of the deceased consumer`

§§ `Part 3 -- Who may ask for access (ss 10 (8), 10A, 25, 26, 27, dictionary "consumer")`

-- s 10 (8): a legally incompetent consumer's right is exercisable by a guardian. s 10A: a child's by the person with
-- parental responsibility; a young person's personally if of sufficient maturity and developmental capacity, otherwise by
-- the person with parental responsibility. s 27 (2): a deceased consumer's by the legal representative. Dictionary,
-- "consumer" (d), and s 13B (3) (c) (ii) (B): if there is no legal representative, an immediate family member is the
-- consumer. s 25 (b) says a child's or young person's right "is not exercisable by the consumer on the consumer's own
-- behalf", but s 25 is "Subject to this Act", and s 10A (b) (i) gives the mature young person the right personally
-- (finding 1).
@export Who can ask for access to the health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 10 (8), 10A, 25-27)? An adult asks personally; a guardian asks for a legally incompetent person; the person with parental responsibility asks for a child under 12, and for a young person who lacks the maturity to understand the request and the record (a mature young person asks personally); for a consumer who has died, the executor or administrator asks, or, if there is none, an immediate family member.
GIVEN `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is` @desc The situation of the person the health record is about (the consumer): an adult, a legally incompetent person (under an operative enduring power of attorney or an adult guardianship order), a child under 12, a young person (12 to 17) with or without the maturity to understand the request and the record, or someone who has died, with or without an executor (with probate) or administrator
GIVETH A `Who acts`
`ss 10A, 25-27 -- who can ask for access` `the consumer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the consumer`
    WHEN `an adult who is not a legally incompetent person` THEN `the consumer personally`
    WHEN `a legally incompetent person` THEN `a guardian of the consumer`
    WHEN `a child (under 12)` THEN `the person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `a young person with sufficient maturity and developmental capacity to understand the request and the record` THEN `the consumer personally`
    WHEN `a young person without that maturity and capacity` THEN `the person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is a legal representative` THEN `the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is no legal representative` THEN `an immediate family member of the deceased consumer`

§§ `Part 3 -- The right of access (ss 10, 14, 14A, 15, 17)`

DECLARE `What the record is` IS ONE OF
    `a record held by a health service provider`
    `a record held by someone else that contains personal health information about me`
    `a record held by someone else that contains no personal health information about me`

-- Dictionary, "factual matter": a history of the health, an illness or a disability; findings on an examination;
-- results of an investigation; a diagnosis or preliminary diagnosis; a plan of management of treatment or care; any
-- action taken by or under the direction or referral of a health service provider. Anything else is matter of opinion.
DECLARE `What this part contains` IS ONE OF
    `factual matters (history, examination findings, test results, diagnosis, treatment plan, action taken)`
    `matters of opinion`

-- s 17 (2): who gave material in confidence to the person who wrote the record.
DECLARE `Given in confidence by` IS ONE OF
    `nothing in this part was given in confidence`
    `me, my guardian or a person with parental responsibility for me`
    `a health service provider in the course of, or in relation to, treating me`
    `someone else (not me, my guardian, a parent or a treating provider)`

-- s 17 (2), (3): a part of a record is "subject to confidentiality" if it includes material given in confidence to the
-- writer by someone other than the consumer, a guardian, a person with parental responsibility, or a health service
-- provider in relation to the consumer's treatment; or if the consumer asked a record keeper to keep it confidential,
-- the record keeper marked it, and the consumer then became legally incompetent or died. s 17 (4): for (3), a deceased
-- consumer's "consumer" does not include the legal representative. Both are "if" conditions: the text's no otherwise.
GIVEN `given in confidence by` IS A `Given in confidence by`, `I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `I have since become legally incompetent or died` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 17 -- the part is subject to confidentiality` `given in confidence by` `I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` `I have since become legally incompetent or died` IF
        `given in confidence by` EQUALS `someone else (not me, my guardian, a parent or a treating provider)`
    OR  (`I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` AND `I have since become legally incompetent or died`)

DECLARE `Access to this part` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you have a right of access to this part (s 10 (1)), and none of ss 10 (2), 14, 14A, 15 or 17 stands in the way`
    `NOTHING-kind: s 10 (1) gives no right of access to a record held by someone other than a health service provider that contains no personal health information about you`
    `no (the text's no): this part is matter of opinion recorded before 1 February 1998 (s 10 (1) (d)); factual matters in it can still be seen`
    `no: access would contravene a territory or Commonwealth law or a court order (s 10 (2) (a), s 14 (c))`
    `the record keeper may refuse: it does not have possession, custody or control of this part (s 14 (a))`
    `the record keeper may refuse: this part does not relate to you in any respect (s 14 (b))`
    `no: the record keeper must not give access -- it relates to a child protection report and identifies the reporter (s 14A)`
    `no: the record keeper must not give access -- it believes on reasonable grounds that the information would be a significant risk to life or health (s 15); see ss 16-16D if the risk is to you`
    `no: the record keeper must not give access -- this part is subject to confidentiality (s 17)`

-- s 10 (1): a right of access to (a) a record held by a health service provider, or (b) to the extent it contains the
-- consumer's personal health information, any other health record; (c) factual matters whenever made; (d) matters of
-- opinion "if the record was created on or after the date of commencement of this Act". s 10 (2): not where access
-- would contravene a law of the Territory or the Commonwealth or a court order (including a subpoena), nor where s 14,
-- 14A, 15 or 17 applies. Order of the answers: scope first, then the s 10 (1) (d) date, then the law or court order,
-- then the s 14 grounds, then the prohibitions. Fork F4: s 10 (1) (b) failing is NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its
-- own wording); s 10 (1) (d) failing is the text's no (an "if").
@export May I see this part of my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 10, 14, 14A, 15, 17)? You have a right of access to a health record held by a health service provider, and to any other record so far as it holds your personal health information: factual matters whenever recorded, opinions only if recorded on or after 1 February 1998. Access is withheld where it would breach a law or court order, where the record keeper does not have the record or it does not relate to you, and must not be given for a child protection report identifying the reporter, a significant risk to life or health, or material given in confidence.
GIVEN `what the record is` IS A `What the record is` @desc Who holds the record and whether it holds personal health information about you (a doctor, hospital, pharmacy, dentist, psychologist and the like are health service providers)
      `what this part contains` IS A `What this part contains` @desc Whether the part you want is factual (health history, examination findings, test results, diagnosis, treatment plan, action taken) or a matter of opinion
      `this part was made on` IS A DATE @desc The day this part of the record was created (it matters only for opinions: opinions recorded before 1 February 1998 are not covered)
      `access would breach a law or court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving access would contravene an ACT law (other than this Act), a Commonwealth law, or a court order or subpoena
      `the record keeper has it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you asked actually has possession, custody or control of this part of the record
      `it relates to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this part relates to you in any respect
      `a child protection report that identifies the reporter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this part relates to a child concern, voluntary, mandatory or prenatal report (or care and protection report information) under the children and young people laws, and identifies the person who made the report, or the record keeper is satisfied that their identity could be worked out from it
      `the record keeper believes it would be a significant risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper believes, on reasonable grounds, that giving the information would be a significant risk to the life or physical, mental or emotional health of you or anyone else
      `given in confidence by` IS A `Given in confidence by` @desc Who, if anyone, gave material in this part to the person who wrote it in confidence
      `I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the consumer) told a record keeper you wanted this part kept confidential and the record keeper marked it so
      `I have since become legally incompetent or died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after that, the consumer became a legally incompetent person or died
GIVETH A `Access to this part`
`s 10 -- may I see this part of my health record` `what the record is` `what this part contains` `this part was made on` `access would breach a law or court order` `the record keeper has it` `it relates to me` `a child protection report that identifies the reporter` `the record keeper believes it would be a significant risk` `given in confidence by` `I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` `I have since become legally incompetent or died` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `what the record is` EQUALS `a record held by someone else that contains no personal health information about me`
        THEN `NOTHING-kind: s 10 (1) gives no right of access to a record held by someone other than a health service provider that contains no personal health information about you`
        IF `what this part contains` EQUALS `matters of opinion` AND `this part was made on` `falls before` `the date of commencement of this Act`
        THEN `no (the text's no): this part is matter of opinion recorded before 1 February 1998 (s 10 (1) (d)); factual matters in it can still be seen`
        IF `access would breach a law or court order`
        THEN `no: access would contravene a territory or Commonwealth law or a court order (s 10 (2) (a), s 14 (c))`
        IF (NOT (`the record keeper has it`))
        THEN `the record keeper may refuse: it does not have possession, custody or control of this part (s 14 (a))`
        IF (NOT (`it relates to me`))
        THEN `the record keeper may refuse: this part does not relate to you in any respect (s 14 (b))`
        IF `a child protection report that identifies the reporter`
        THEN `no: the record keeper must not give access -- it relates to a child protection report and identifies the reporter (s 14A)`
        IF `the record keeper believes it would be a significant risk`
        THEN `no: the record keeper must not give access -- it believes on reasonable grounds that the information would be a significant risk to life or health (s 15); see ss 16-16D if the risk is to you`
        IF `s 17 -- the part is subject to confidentiality` `given in confidence by` `I asked for it to stay confidential and it was marked` `I have since become legally incompetent or died`
        THEN `no: the record keeper must not give access -- this part is subject to confidentiality (s 17)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you have a right of access to this part (s 10 (1)), and none of ss 10 (2), 14, 14A, 15 or 17 stands in the way`

DECLARE `Way of access` IS ONE OF
    `inspect the record (or a print-out of an electronic record) and take notes`
    `receive a copy of the record`
    `view the record and have it explained`

DECLARE `Fee for access` IS ONE OF
    `no fee: the right to inspect is not subject to a fee (s 10 (7))`
    `a fee may be charged, if the Minister has determined one under s 34, and access can be withheld until it is paid (s 10 (7), s 13 (2) (d) (ii))`

-- s 10 (7): "The right of access is subject, in the case of access in a way mentioned in subsection (3) (b) or (c), to
-- the payment of any fee or charge payable under this Act." Inspection is not made subject to a fee. The s 34 fee
-- determination is not deposited.
@export Do I have to pay to see my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 10 (3), (7))? Inspecting the record is not subject to a fee. Getting a copy, or viewing the record and having it explained, is subject to any fee the Minister has determined under s 34, not more than the determined fee.
GIVEN `the way I asked for` IS A `Way of access` @desc The way of access you asked for: inspecting the record and taking notes, receiving a copy, or viewing it and having it explained by a suitably qualified health service provider
GIVETH A `Fee for access`
`s 10 (7) -- do I have to pay for access` `the way I asked for` MEANS
    IF `the way I asked for` EQUALS `inspect the record (or a print-out of an electronic record) and take notes`
    THEN `no fee: the right to inspect is not subject to a fee (s 10 (7))`
    ELSE `a fee may be charged, if the Minister has determined one under s 34, and access can be withheld until it is paid (s 10 (7), s 13 (2) (d) (ii))`

DECLARE `How access is given` IS ONE OF
    `the record (or a print-out) is made available to you at a time, and a place in the ACT, stated in a written notice (s 13C (2) (a))`
    `you get a copy; or an accurate summary if you agree; or, for a part made before 1 February 1998 that is not copied, a written summary of its factual matters (s 13C (2) (b))`
    `you get a written notice naming when and where in the ACT the record keeper will explain the record, or naming a suitably qualified provider in the ACT who will explain it by arrangement with you (s 13C (2) (c))`

-- s 13C (2): how access must be given, for each way of access.
@export How must I be given access to my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 13C)? To inspect: the record or a print-out is made available at a time and place in the ACT stated in a written notice. For a copy: a copy, or an accurate summary if you agree (for a pre-1998 part not copied, a written summary of its factual matters). To have it explained: a written notice of when and where the record keeper will explain it, or the name and address of a suitably qualified provider in the ACT who will.
GIVEN `the way I asked for` IS A `Way of access` @desc The way of access you asked for: inspecting the record and taking notes, receiving a copy, or viewing it and having it explained
GIVETH A `How access is given`
`s 13C -- how must I be given access` `the way I asked for` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the way I asked for`
    WHEN `inspect the record (or a print-out of an electronic record) and take notes` THEN `the record (or a print-out) is made available to you at a time, and a place in the ACT, stated in a written notice (s 13C (2) (a))`
    WHEN `receive a copy of the record` THEN `you get a copy; or an accurate summary if you agree; or, for a part made before 1 February 1998 that is not copied, a written summary of its factual matters (s 13C (2) (b))`
    WHEN `view the record and have it explained` THEN `you get a written notice naming when and where in the ACT the record keeper will explain the record, or naming a suitably qualified provider in the ACT who will explain it by arrangement with you (s 13C (2) (c))`

§§ `Part 3 -- Asking for access (s 12)`

DECLARE `Who is asking` IS ONE OF
    `I am the consumer, asking personally`
    `a person with parental responsibility for a child or young person`
    `a guardian of a legally incompetent person`
    `a legal representative of a deceased consumer`
    `an immediate family member of a deceased consumer with no legal representative`

DECLARE `Must the request be in writing` IS ONE OF
    `yes: this request must be in writing (s 12 (2) (d))`
    `no, it may be oral; but the record keeper may ask for it in writing and need do nothing more on the oral request until then (s 12 (3))`

-- s 12 (2) (d): a request must be in writing if it is (i) by a person with parental responsibility, unless in the 12
-- months before the request that person consented to the treatment that gave rise to the record; (ii) by a guardian of
-- a legally incompetent person; (iii) by a legal representative of a deceased consumer. An immediate family member of a
-- deceased consumer is not in the list (finding 2).
@export Must my request for access to a health record be in writing (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 12 (2) (d), (3))? Yes if you ask as a guardian of a legally incompetent person, as the executor or administrator of someone who has died, or as a parent (or other person with parental responsibility) unless you consented to the treatment behind the record in the 12 months before asking. Otherwise you may ask orally, but the record keeper may ask you to put it in writing.
GIVEN `who is asking` IS A `Who is asking` @desc Who is making the request: the consumer personally, a person with parental responsibility for a child or young person, a guardian of a legally incompetent person, the legal representative (executor or administrator) of a consumer who has died, or an immediate family member of a deceased consumer with no legal representative
      `I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you ask as a person with parental responsibility: whether, in the 12 months before the request, you consented to the treatment of the child or young person that gave rise to the record (otherwise answer no; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Must the request be in writing`
`s 12 (2) (d) -- must my request be in writing` `who is asking` `I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months` MEANS
    IF `writing is required` THEN `yes: this request must be in writing (s 12 (2) (d))`
    ELSE `no, it may be oral; but the record keeper may ask for it in writing and need do nothing more on the oral request until then (s 12 (3))`
    WHERE
        `writing is required` MEANS
                (`who is asking` EQUALS `a person with parental responsibility for a child or young person` AND (NOT (`I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months`)))
            OR  `who is asking` EQUALS `a guardian of a legally incompetent person`
            OR  `who is asking` EQUALS `a legal representative of a deceased consumer`

DECLARE `Does my request start the clock` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the request is properly made, and the record keeper must answer within 2 weeks after receiving it (s 13 (2))`
    `no: the request does not have everything s 12 (2) requires (name, address unless known, the record identified, the way of access, writing where required)`
    `no: it was oral and the record keeper asked you to make it in writing, so nothing more need be done on the oral request (s 12 (3), s 13 (1) (b))`

-- s 12 (2): "A request must -- (a) state the name and, unless it is already known to the record keeper, the address,
-- of the consumer; and (b) sufficiently identify the health record ...; and (c) specify the way ...; and (d) be in
-- writing if ...". s 13 (1) (b): s 13 does not apply to an oral request the record keeper has asked to be put in
-- writing. Fork F5 (a bare "must" not done, decided case by case): a request missing a requirement does not start the
-- s 13 clock.
@export Is my request for access to a health record properly made, so that the record keeper must answer it within 2 weeks (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 12, 13 (1))? The request must give the consumer's name (and address, unless the record keeper already knows it), identify the record, say which way of access you want, and be in writing where s 12 (2) (d) requires. If you asked orally and the record keeper asked you to put it in writing, nothing more need be done on the oral request.
GIVEN `states the name` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request states the consumer's name
      `states the address or it is known` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request states the consumer's address, or the record keeper already knows it
      `identifies the record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request identifies the health record well enough
      `says the way of access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request says which way of access you want (inspect, copy, or view and have it explained)
      `who is asking` IS A `Who is asking` @desc Who is making the request
      `I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you ask as a person with parental responsibility: whether you consented in the 12 months before the request to the treatment that gave rise to the record (otherwise answer no)
      `made in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request was made in writing
      `I was asked to put it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the request was oral: whether the record keeper asked you to make it in writing
GIVETH A `Does my request start the clock`
`s 12 -- is my request properly made` `states the name` `states the address or it is known` `identifies the record` `says the way of access` `who is asking` `I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months` `made in writing` `I was asked to put it in writing` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`made in writing`)) AND `I was asked to put it in writing`
        THEN `no: it was oral and the record keeper asked you to make it in writing, so nothing more need be done on the oral request (s 12 (3), s 13 (1) (b))`
        IF `states the name` AND `states the address or it is known` AND `identifies the record` AND `says the way of access` AND `writing is satisfied`
        THEN `yes: the request is properly made, and the record keeper must answer within 2 weeks after receiving it (s 13 (2))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the request does not have everything s 12 (2) requires (name, address unless known, the record identified, the way of access, writing where required)`
    WHERE
        `writing is satisfied` MEANS
                `made in writing`
            OR  `s 12 (2) (d) -- must my request be in writing` `who is asking` `I consented to the treatment in the last 12 months` EQUALS `no, it may be oral; but the record keeper may ask for it in writing and need do nothing more on the oral request until then (s 12 (3))`

§§ `Part 3 -- The answer, the access, and refusal (ss 9, 13, 13B, 13BA, 13D)`

-- s 13 (2): the answer within 2 weeks after the day the record keeper receives the request; s 9, a longer period agreed.
@export By when must the record keeper answer my request for access (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 13 (2), 9)? Within 2 weeks after the day it receives the request: by giving access, or a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee, or a notice that it refuses (stating the ground) or is prohibited (stating the section), or, for the health services commissioner, a notice that the request has been referred. You and the record keeper may agree a longer period.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your request for access
      `we agreed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the record keeper agreed that the answer could be given within a longer period (s 9)
      `the agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day you agreed (if you did not agree one, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 13 -- by when must my request be answered` `received on` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day` MEANS
    `with any s 9 agreement` (`received on` `plus days` 14) `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day`

-- s 13B (2) and s 13BA (2) (the same days for a child or young person): (a) if a fee notice under s 13 (2) (d) (ii) was
-- given and the fee paid, no later than the later of 1 week after the fee is paid and 30 days after the request was
-- received; (b) if no fee notice was given, no later than 30 days after the request was received. A fee notice given and
-- the fee not paid: no day is set yet.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE, `a fee notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN, `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN, `paid on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`the s 13B day` `received on` `a fee notice was given` `the fee was paid` `paid on` MEANS
    IF `a fee notice was given`
    THEN IF `the fee was paid`
         THEN `due by` (`the later of` (`paid on` `plus days` 7) `and` (`received on` `plus days` 30))
         ELSE `no deadline yet: the fee stated in the notice has not been paid`
    ELSE `due by` (`received on` `plus days` 30)

@export By when must I be given access to my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 13B (2), 13BA (2), 9)? No later than 30 days after the day the record keeper received your request; or, if it told you access would be given on payment of a fee and you paid, no later than the later of 1 week after you paid and 30 days after the request. Until a fee you were told of is paid, no day is set. You and the record keeper may agree a longer period. Unless you were given a fee notice, though, s 13 (2) (d) (i) makes access itself the answer due within 2 weeks (fork F14).
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your request for access
      `a fee notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper gave you written notice that it will give access on payment of a stated fee
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid that fee
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `we agreed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the record keeper agreed that access could be given within a longer period (s 9)
      `the agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day you agreed (if you did not agree one, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`ss 13B, 13BA -- by when must I be given access` `received on` `a fee notice was given` `the fee was paid` `paid on` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the s 13B day` `received on` `a fee notice was given` `the fee was paid` `paid on`
    WHEN `due by` d THEN `due by` (`with any s 9 agreement` d `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day`)
    OTHERWISE `no deadline yet: the fee stated in the notice has not been paid`

-- s 13 (2) lists the answers the record keeper must give within 2 weeks: (a) a referral notice (the commissioner);
-- (b) a notice of nonproduction on a s 14 ground; (c) a notice that s 14A, 15 or 17 prohibits access; (d) (i) access
-- itself, or (ii) a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee. A notice that access will be given
-- later, with no fee, is not on the list. Fork F14: s 13 (2) (d) (i) is read as written, so where no fee notice is
-- given, access itself is the answer due within 2 weeks, and a record keeper that has given neither access nor one of
-- the notices by then is taken to have refused (s 13D (a) (i)), though s 13B (2) (b) would allow 30 days (finding 12).
DECLARE `The answer I got` IS ONE OF
    `no answer yet`
    `access given`
    `a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee`
    `a notice refusing access to the whole record on a s 14 ground, or saying s 14A, 15 or 17 forbids access`
    `a notice that the health services commissioner has referred the request to the provider who prepared the record`

DECLARE `Is access taken to have been refused` IS ONE OF
    `yes: no answer within 2 weeks after the request was received (s 13 (2); s 13D (a) (i)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
    `yes: access was not given by the day s 13B (2) sets (s 13D (a) (iii)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
    `NOTHING-kind: access to a child or young person was not given by the day s 13BA (2) sets, a breach of s 13BA (2); but s 13D (a) lists only ss 13, 13A, 13B and 13C, so the text does not say access is taken to have been refused (finding 11)`
    `yes: the arrangements to inspect the record or have it explained were unreasonable (s 13D (b)-(d)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
    `refused, not just taken to be: the record keeper refused by notice (s 13 (2) (b), (c)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
    `not refused: the record keeper need not give access, because the evidence of identity or authority it asked for was not given or did not satisfy it (s 13B (4), s 13BA (6))`
    `not refused: the request was referred to the provider who prepared the record, which is taken to have received it under s 12 (s 10 (6)); ask again about that provider`
    `not refused: access was given in time`
    `not yet: the time has not run out`
    `not yet: you have been told of a fee, and no day for access runs until it is paid`

-- s 13D: a record keeper "is taken to have refused access" if it (a) fails to comply with a requirement of s 13, 13A,
-- 13B or 13C, or (b)-(d) the notice of a time to inspect or explain is unreasonable or it (or the provider named)
-- unreasonably fails to follow it. The two time limits are run as regulative rules: s 13 (2) answer within 2 weeks
-- (fork F14: where there is no fee notice, the answer is access itself), and s 13B (2) access by the day it sets after a
-- fee notice. s 13BA (2) sets the same days for a child or young person, but s 13D (a) does not list s 13BA: late access
-- to a child or young person under s 13BA is a breach the text does not deem a refusal (NOTHING-kind; finding 11, fork
-- F15). s 13B (4), s 13BA (6): if the record keeper asked for evidence of identity or authority and it was not given or
-- did not satisfy it, the record keeper "need not provide access" (fork F6: not a refusal; it relieves the giving of
-- access only, so it is checked after the s 13 (2) answer day). Whether the arrangements were unreasonable is a
-- judgment: asked.
@export Is the record keeper taken to have refused me access to my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 13, 13B, 13BA, 13D)? A record keeper that within 2 weeks neither gives access nor gives a fee, refusal, prohibition or referral notice, that does not give access by the fee day after a fee notice, or that makes unreasonable arrangements for inspection or explanation, is taken to have refused access, and you can complain to the Human Rights Commission. Not if it asked for evidence of identity or authority that was not given. The Act does not say that late access to a child or young person (s 13BA) is taken to be a refusal.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your request for access (properly made)
      `the answer I got` IS A `The answer I got` @desc What the record keeper has done in answer to your request so far: nothing yet, given access, a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee, a notice refusing access (or saying it is prohibited), or a notice that the request was referred
      `answered on` IS A DATE @desc The day you got that answer; for access given, the day access was given (if no answer yet, any date; it is not used)
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the answer was a fee notice: whether you have paid the fee (otherwise answer no; it is not used)
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `access was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have actually been given access (inspection made available, a copy given, or the explanation arranged as s 13C requires)
      `access given on` IS A DATE @desc The day access was given (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `access is to a child or young person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person to be given access is a child (under 12) or young person, so that s 13BA rather than s 13B sets the access day
      `evidence asked for was not given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper asked for evidence of your identity or authority, and it was not given or did not satisfy the record keeper
      `the arrangements were unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a notice did not state a reasonable time to inspect or have the record explained, or the record keeper (or the provider it named) unreasonably failed to make the record available, explain it, or make or keep an arrangement to explain it
      `we agreed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the record keeper agreed a longer period for the answer and the access (s 9)
      `the agreed answer day` IS A DATE @desc The last day agreed for the answer (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `the agreed access day` IS A DATE @desc The last day agreed for access (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Is access taken to have been refused`
`s 13D -- is access taken to have been refused` `received on` `the answer I got` `answered on` `the fee was paid` `paid on` `access was given` `access given on` `access is to a child or young person` `evidence asked for was not given` `the arrangements were unreasonable` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed answer day` `the agreed access day` `today` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `answer timing` EQUALS `out of time`
        THEN `yes: no answer within 2 weeks after the request was received (s 13 (2); s 13D (a) (i)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
        IF `evidence asked for was not given`
        THEN `not refused: the record keeper need not give access, because the evidence of identity or authority it asked for was not given or did not satisfy it (s 13B (4), s 13BA (6))`
        IF `the answer I got` EQUALS `a notice refusing access to the whole record on a s 14 ground, or saying s 14A, 15 or 17 forbids access`
        THEN `refused, not just taken to be: the record keeper refused by notice (s 13 (2) (b), (c)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
        IF `the answer I got` EQUALS `a notice that the health services commissioner has referred the request to the provider who prepared the record`
        THEN `not refused: the request was referred to the provider who prepared the record, which is taken to have received it under s 12 (s 10 (6)); ask again about that provider`
        IF `the arrangements were unreasonable`
        THEN `yes: the arrangements to inspect the record or have it explained were unreasonable (s 13D (b)-(d)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
        IF `access timing` EQUALS JUST `out of time` AND `access is to a child or young person`
        THEN `NOTHING-kind: access to a child or young person was not given by the day s 13BA (2) sets, a breach of s 13BA (2); but s 13D (a) lists only ss 13, 13A, 13B and 13C, so the text does not say access is taken to have been refused (finding 11)`
        IF `access timing` EQUALS JUST `out of time`
        THEN `yes: access was not given by the day s 13B (2) sets (s 13D (a) (iii)); you may complain (s 18 (1) (b), (c))`
        IF `access timing` EQUALS JUST `done in time`
        THEN `not refused: access was given in time`
        IF `access timing` EQUALS NOTHING
        THEN `not yet: you have been told of a fee, and no day for access runs until it is paid`
        OTHERWISE `not yet: the time has not run out`
    WHERE
        `a fee notice was given` MEANS `the answer I got` EQUALS `a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee`
        `the answer day` MEANS `s 13 -- by when must my request be answered` `received on` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed answer day`
        `answered` MEANS (NOT (`the answer I got` EQUALS `no answer yet`)) OR `access was given`
        `the day answered` MEANS IF `the answer I got` EQUALS `no answer yet` THEN `access given on` ELSE `answered on`
        `answer timing` MEANS `the timing under` (`s 13 (2) -- answer the request within 2 weeks` (`the days from` `received on` `to` `the answer day`)) `the record keeper` `answer the request for access` `answered` `the day answered` `today` `received on` `the answer day`
        `access timing` MEANS
            CONSIDER `ss 13B, 13BA -- by when must I be given access` `received on` `a fee notice was given` `the fee was paid` `paid on` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed access day`
            WHEN `due by` d THEN JUST (`the timing under` (`s 13B (2) -- give access by the last day` (`the days from` `received on` `to` d)) `the record keeper` `give access to the record` `access was given` `access given on` `today` `received on` d)
            OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 13 (5): "If the record keeper gives a notice under subsection (2) (b) or (c) that relates to only part of the record,
-- the record keeper must comply with subsection (2) (d) in relation to the rest of the record": access to the rest, or a
-- fee notice for it, within the same 2 weeks (s 9 may lengthen it). An "if": the text's no for a notice about the whole
-- record.
@export If the record keeper refused (or said it is prohibited from giving) access to only part of my health record, must I still get the rest, and by when (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 13 (5), (2) (d), s 9)? Yes: for the rest of the record it must, within 2 weeks after the day it received your request, give you access or a notice that access will be given on payment of a stated fee. You and the record keeper may agree a longer period.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your request for access
      `the notice covers only part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the refusal or prohibition notice relates to only part of the record (no if it covers the whole record)
      `we agreed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the record keeper agreed a longer period (s 9)
      `the agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day you agreed (if you did not agree one, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`s 13 (5) -- must I still be given the rest of my record` `received on` `the notice covers only part` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day` MEANS
    IF `the notice covers only part`
    THEN `due by` (`s 13 -- by when must my request be answered` `received on` `we agreed a longer period` `the agreed last day`)
    ELSE `no duty on these facts (the text's no)`

§§ `Part 3 -- Disclosure to someone I choose (s 13A, schedule 1 principle 10 cl 2 (c), cl 7)`

DECLARE `Who signed` IS ONE OF
    `signed by the consumer personally`
    `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    `signed by a guardian of the consumer`
    `signed by the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    `signed by an immediate family member of the deceased consumer`
    `signed by someone else`

-- Principle 10 cl 7: a consent for cl 2 (c) must (a) be in writing and signed (i) for a child or young person without
-- sufficient maturity and developmental capacity, by a person with parental responsibility; (ii) for a legally
-- incompetent person, by a guardian; (iii) in any other case, by the consumer; and (b) name the health service provider
-- who made the record. Fork F7: "a child or young person who does not have sufficient maturity" is read as every child
-- under 12 and the young person without that maturity. A deceased consumer's consent is given by the legal representative
-- (s 27 (2); the dictionary's "consumer" (c)); with no legal representative, fork F8: cl 7 (a) (iii) "in any other case,
-- by the consumer", and the dictionary's "consumer" (d) includes the immediate family member, as for access (s 13B (3)
-- (c) (ii) (B)). A consent may still be void against the person's earlier wishes (s 26 (2), s 27 (3)): asked apart.
GIVEN `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is`
GIVETH A `Who signed`
`the right signer under principle 10 cl 7` `the consumer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the consumer`
    WHEN `a child (under 12)` THEN `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `a young person without that maturity and capacity` THEN `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `a legally incompetent person` THEN `signed by a guardian of the consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is a legal representative` THEN `signed by the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is no legal representative` THEN `signed by an immediate family member of the deceased consumer`
    OTHERWISE `signed by the consumer personally`

@export Is my consent to the disclosure of my health record to someone I choose valid (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 10 cl 7)? It must be in writing; signed by you, or by a person with parental responsibility for a child or a young person who lacks the maturity to understand, or by a guardian of a legally incompetent person (for someone who has died, by the executor or administrator, or, if there is none, an immediate family member); and it must name the health service provider who made the record.
GIVEN `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is` @desc The situation of the person the record is about (adult, legally incompetent, child under 12, young person with or without the maturity to understand, or dead with or without an executor or administrator)
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent is in writing
      `signed by` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the consent
      `names the provider who made the record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent names the health service provider who made the record
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `principle 10 cl 7 -- is my consent to disclosure valid` `the consumer` `in writing` `signed by` `names the provider who made the record` IF
        `in writing`
    AND (NOT (`signed by` EQUALS `signed by someone else`))
    AND `signed by` EQUALS `the right signer under principle 10 cl 7` `the consumer`
    AND `names the provider who made the record`

DECLARE `Who gave the consent` IS ONE OF
    `the consumer personally`
    `a guardian of the consumer (a legally incompetent person)`
    `the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    `an immediate family member of the deceased consumer (no legal representative)`
    `a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`

-- s 26 (2): "A purported consent by a guardian of a legally incompetent person is void if, when giving it, the guardian
-- knows or believes it to be at variance with the wishes expressed, and not changed or withdrawn, by the person before the
-- person became a legally incompetent person." s 27 (3): the same for a legal representative of a deceased consumer and
-- the wishes expressed "in the consumer's lifetime". Each is an "if": the text's no otherwise. Neither reaches an
-- immediate family member signing under the dictionary's "consumer" (d), nor a person with parental responsibility
-- (finding 14). What the signer knew or believed is asked.
@export Is a consent given for me by my guardian, or after my death by my executor or administrator, void because it goes against wishes I had expressed (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 26 (2), 27 (3))? Yes, if when giving it the guardian (or legal representative) knew or believed it to be at variance with wishes the person expressed before becoming legally incompetent (or in their lifetime) and had not changed or withdrawn. The Act says nothing like this for an immediate family member or a parent.
GIVEN `who gave it` IS A `Who gave the consent` @desc Who gave the consent on the consumer's behalf
      `wishes were expressed and not withdrawn` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consumer expressed wishes about it (for a guardian: before becoming a legally incompetent person; for a legal representative: in their lifetime), and did not change or withdraw them
      `the signer knew or believed it went against them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when giving the consent, the guardian or legal representative knew or believed it to be at variance with those wishes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 26 (2), 27 (3) -- is the consent given for me void` `who gave it` `wishes were expressed and not withdrawn` `the signer knew or believed it went against them` IF
        (`who gave it` EQUALS `a guardian of the consumer (a legally incompetent person)` OR `who gave it` EQUALS `the legal representative of the deceased consumer`)
    AND `wishes were expressed and not withdrawn`
    AND `the signer knew or believed it went against them`

-- s 13A: if the consumer has a right of access and consents under principle 10 cl 2 (c) to disclosure to an entity
-- (for example the consumer's lawyer), "the record keeper must disclose the information to the entity", and the entity
-- is taken to be the consumer, with the same right of access. "This section applies if": the text's no otherwise.
@export Must the record keeper give my health information to the person or organisation I consent to (for example my lawyer) (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 13A, schedule 1 principle 10 cl 2 (c), cl 7)? Yes, if you have a right of access to the record and you give a valid written consent to the disclosure; the person you name then has the same right of access as you.
GIVEN `I have a right of access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a right of access to the record (for example because the "May I see this part of my health record" question answered yes)
      `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is` @desc The situation of the person the record is about
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent is in writing
      `signed by` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the consent
      `names the provider who made the record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent names the health service provider who made the record
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 13A -- must my record be disclosed to the entity I consent to` `I have a right of access` `the consumer` `in writing` `signed by` `names the provider who made the record` IF
        `I have a right of access`
    AND `principle 10 cl 7 -- is my consent to disclosure valid` `the consumer` `in writing` `signed by` `names the provider who made the record`

§§ `Part 3 -- When access is refused for a risk to my health (ss 16A, 16B, 16D) -- only if the record keeper refused on the s 15 (a) ground`

DECLARE `The record keeper's offer` IS ONE OF
    `no offer to discuss the record was made`
    `an offer was made and I did not accept it`
    `I discussed the record with the provider named in the offer and am not satisfied`
    `I discussed the record with the provider named in the offer and am satisfied`

-- s 16A (1), (2): if the record keeper gave a s 13 (2) (c) notice on the s 15 (a) ground (risk to the consumer) and either
-- made no s 16 offer, or the offer was not accepted, or the consumer discussed the record and is not satisfied, the
-- consumer "may give a written notice ... nominating a health service provider ... if the health service provider has
-- consented to being nominated". "This section applies if" and "if": the text's no otherwise.
@export Can I nominate a health service provider of my choice to look at the health record I was refused because of a risk to my own health (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 16A)? Yes, if the record keeper refused because giving it would be a significant risk to your life or health, and it made no offer to have a provider discuss the record with you, or you did not accept the offer, or you discussed it and are not satisfied; the provider you nominate must have consented, and the nomination is a written notice.
GIVEN `refused for a risk to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper gave you notice that it is prohibited from giving access because of a significant risk to your own life or health (s 15 (a)), as opposed to a risk to someone else
      `the offer` IS A `The record keeper's offer` @desc Whether the record keeper offered to have the record discussed with you by a suitably qualified provider, and what happened
      `the provider consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service provider you want to nominate has consented to being nominated
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your nomination is a written notice to the record keeper
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 16A -- can I nominate a provider to look at my record` `refused for a risk to me` `the offer` `the provider consented` `in writing` IF
        `refused for a risk to me`
    AND (NOT (`the offer` EQUALS `I discussed the record with the provider named in the offer and am satisfied`))
    AND `the provider consented`
    AND `in writing`

DECLARE `My nominated provider` IS ONE OF
    `is registered with the same national board as the provider who wrote the record`
    `is a registered medical practitioner able to understand and interpret the record`
    `provides the same kind of service as the provider who wrote the record, and is able to understand and interpret it`
    `none of these`

-- s 16D (1), (2), (4): the record keeper may object within 2 weeks if it believes on reasonable grounds the nominee is
-- not suitable, but "must not" object if the nominee is registered with the same national board as the writer, is a
-- registered medical practitioner able to understand and interpret the record, or provides the same kind of service and
-- is able to understand and interpret it. The flat negative is the text's no; the belief is asked.
@export Can the record keeper object to the health service provider I nominated (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 16D)? Only within 2 weeks after receiving the nomination, only if it believes on reasonable grounds the provider is unsuitable, and never if your provider is registered with the same national board as the one who wrote the record, is a registered medical practitioner able to understand and interpret it, or provides the same kind of service and can understand and interpret it.
GIVEN `my nominee` IS A `My nominated provider` @desc Which of these describes the provider you nominated
      `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper believes, on reasonable grounds, that your nominee is not a suitable person to act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 16D -- can the record keeper object to my nominee` `my nominee` `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable` IF
        `my nominee` EQUALS `none of these`
    AND `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable`

DECLARE `Must my nominee get the record` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the record (or a copy) must be given to your nominee within 2 weeks after the nomination was received (s 16B (2))`
    `no (the text's no): the record keeper objected to your nominee within the 2 weeks, as s 16D allows (s 16B (3) (a)); you may nominate another`
    `no (the text's no): the nomination lapsed, because the record keeper gave you "a notice under section 16B" (s 16B provides for no notice; probably s 16D is meant: finding 13), or your nominee died, stopped practising, refused or did not act in reasonable time (s 16A (3), s 16B (3) (b)); you may nominate another`
    `yes: the record keeper's objection does not count, because s 16D (4) forbids objecting to this nominee or it was given out of time; the record must be given within 2 weeks (s 16B (2))`

-- s 16B (2), (3): the record keeper must give the record to the nominee within 2 weeks after the nomination, but need not
-- if within that period it gives a s 16D notice or the nomination lapses. Fork F9: an objection s 16D (4) forbids, or
-- one given after the 2 weeks, is not "a notice under section 16D", so the duty stands.
@export Must the record keeper give my nominated health service provider my record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 16A (3), 16B, 16D)? Yes, within 2 weeks after receiving your nomination, unless in that time it objects to your nominee as s 16D allows or the nomination lapses.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your written nomination
      `my nominee` IS A `My nominated provider` @desc Which of these describes the provider you nominated
      `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper believes, on reasonable grounds, that your nominee is not a suitable person to act
      `an objection was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper gave you a written notice objecting to your nominee
      `objection given on` IS A DATE @desc The day it gave the objection (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `the nomination lapsed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, within the 2 weeks, the nomination lapsed (your nominee died, stopped being a health service provider, refused to act or did not act within a reasonable time)
GIVETH A `Must my nominee get the record`
`s 16B -- must my nominee get the record` `received on` `my nominee` `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable` `an objection was given` `objection given on` `the nomination lapsed` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the nomination lapsed`
        THEN `no (the text's no): the nomination lapsed, because the record keeper gave you "a notice under section 16B" (s 16B provides for no notice; probably s 16D is meant: finding 13), or your nominee died, stopped practising, refused or did not act in reasonable time (s 16A (3), s 16B (3) (b)); you may nominate another`
        IF `an objection was given` AND `the objection counts`
        THEN `no (the text's no): the record keeper objected to your nominee within the 2 weeks, as s 16D allows (s 16B (3) (a)); you may nominate another`
        IF `an objection was given`
        THEN `yes: the record keeper's objection does not count, because s 16D (4) forbids objecting to this nominee or it was given out of time; the record must be given within 2 weeks (s 16B (2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the record (or a copy) must be given to your nominee within 2 weeks after the nomination was received (s 16B (2))`
    WHERE
        `the objection counts` MEANS
                `s 16D -- can the record keeper object to my nominee` `my nominee` `the record keeper believes the nominee unsuitable`
            AND (NOT ((`received on` `plus days` 14) `falls before` `objection given on`))

-- The s 16B rule, run: is the record keeper in time?
@export Has the record keeper given my nominated provider the record in time (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 16B (2))? It must give the record, or a copy, to your nominee within 2 weeks after the day it received your nomination (unless it objected as s 16D allows or the nomination lapsed).
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the record keeper received your written nomination
      `given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper has given your nominee the record or a copy
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc The day it did (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Timing`
`s 16B -- has my nominee been given the record in time` `received on` `given` `given on` `today` MEANS
    `the timing under` `s 16B (2) -- give the record to the nominated provider within 2 weeks` `the record keeper` `give the record to the nominated provider` `given` `given on` `today` `received on` (`received on` `plus days` 14)

§§ `Health status reports (s 7) -- for a report about my health that is not for my own care, such as an insurer's, employer's or lawyer's medical report`

DECLARE `What was done with the report` IS ONE OF
    `someone asked a person other than me for a health status report about me`
    `someone gave a health status report about me to a person other than me`
    `someone obtained a health status report about me from a health service provider`

DECLARE `May they do it` IS ONE OF
    `s 7 does not apply: this is not a health status report (the text's no)`
    `s 7 does not apply: it is done in accordance with a territory or Commonwealth law or a court order (s 7 (5))`
    `no (the text's no): not without your consent, and you have not given a valid one (s 7 (1), (2), (4))`
    `yes, with your consent: and they must tell you in writing that you may ask for a copy and give them comments, give you a copy as soon as practicable if you ask, and consider and keep your comments with the report (s 7 (3))`

-- Dictionary, "health status report": a report (a) prepared or substantially prepared by a health service provider about
-- a consumer who, when it is prepared, resides or is present in the ACT; (b) relating to the consumer's physical,
-- mental or emotional health, or a disability or disease; (c) whose purpose, or main purpose, is not a health service
-- for the consumer. A "means" definition: the text's no otherwise.
GIVEN `prepared by a health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN, `I was in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN, `about my health` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `main purpose is not my care` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a health status report` `prepared by a health service provider` `I was in the ACT` `about my health` `main purpose is not my care` IF
        `prepared by a health service provider`
    AND `I was in the ACT`
    AND `about my health`
    AND `main purpose is not my care`

-- s 7 (4): the consent must (a) be in writing and signed: (i) for a legally incompetent person, by a guardian; (ii) for
-- a child or young person, by the person with parental responsibility; (iii) otherwise by the consumer; and (b) name
-- the health service provider if the report is prepared by one (always, by the definition). Here every child and young
-- person signs through the person with parental responsibility (unlike principle 10 cl 7: finding 3). For a deceased
-- consumer the legal representative (s 27 (2)), or, if none, an immediate family member as "the consumer" (dictionary
-- (d); fork F8).
GIVEN `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is`
GIVETH A `Who signed`
`the right signer under s 7 (4)` `the consumer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the consumer`
    WHEN `a legally incompetent person` THEN `signed by a guardian of the consumer`
    WHEN `a child (under 12)` THEN `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `a young person with sufficient maturity and developmental capacity to understand the request and the record` THEN `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `a young person without that maturity and capacity` THEN `signed by a person with parental responsibility for the consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is a legal representative` THEN `signed by the legal representative of the deceased consumer`
    WHEN `dead, and there is no legal representative` THEN `signed by an immediate family member of the deceased consumer`
    OTHERWISE `signed by the consumer personally`

-- s 7 (1): a person must not, without the consumer's consent, (a) ask someone other than the consumer for a health
-- status report about the consumer, or (b) give one to someone other than the consumer; (2) obtain one from a health
-- service provider. (5): not where done in accordance with a territory or Commonwealth law or a court order. Each is a
-- flat negative: without a valid consent, the text's no.
@export Can someone ask for, give out or obtain a health status report about me (a medical report for an insurer, employer, lawyer or the like, not for my own care) without my consent (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 7)? No, unless a law or court order provides for it. Your consent must be in writing, signed (by you; for a child or young person, by a parent; for a legally incompetent person, by a guardian; for someone who has died, by the executor or administrator or, if none, an immediate family member) and name the health service provider. Once you consent, you must be told in writing of your right to a copy and to comment.
GIVEN `prepared by a health service provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report is (or is to be) prepared, or substantially prepared, by a health service provider (a doctor, psychologist, physiotherapist and the like)
      `I was in the ACT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you lived in, or were present in, the ACT when the report was (or is to be) prepared
      `about my health` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report is about your physical, mental or emotional health, or a disability or disease
      `main purpose is not my care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the report's purpose, or main purpose, is something other than a health service for you (for example an insurance, compensation, employment or legal purpose)
      `what was done` IS A `What was done with the report` @desc What the person did (or wants to do) with the report
      `under a law or court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is done in accordance with an ACT or Commonwealth law, or a court order, that provides for it
      `I consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a consent was given
      `the consumer` IS A `The consumer is` @desc The situation of the person the report is about (adult, legally incompetent, child under 12, young person, or dead with or without an executor or administrator)
      `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent is in writing
      `signed by` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the consent
      `names the provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the consent names the health service provider who prepared (or will prepare) the report
GIVETH A `May they do it`
`s 7 -- may they get a health status report about me` `prepared by a health service provider` `I was in the ACT` `about my health` `main purpose is not my care` `what was done` `under a law or court order` `I consented` `the consumer` `in writing` `signed by` `names the provider` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`it is a health status report` `prepared by a health service provider` `I was in the ACT` `about my health` `main purpose is not my care`))
        THEN `s 7 does not apply: this is not a health status report (the text's no)`
        IF `under a law or court order`
        THEN `s 7 does not apply: it is done in accordance with a territory or Commonwealth law or a court order (s 7 (5))`
        IF `I consented` AND `in writing` AND (NOT (`signed by` EQUALS `signed by someone else`)) AND `signed by` EQUALS `the right signer under s 7 (4)` `the consumer` AND `names the provider`
        THEN `yes, with your consent: and they must tell you in writing that you may ask for a copy and give them comments, give you a copy as soon as practicable if you ask, and consider and keep your comments with the report (s 7 (3))`
        OTHERWISE `no (the text's no): not without your consent, and you have not given a valid one (s 7 (1), (2), (4))`

-- s 7 (3) (b): "if the consumer asks for a copy of the report -- give a copy of the report to the consumer as soon as
-- practicable". The duty arises where the person obtained the consumer's consent for s 7 (1) or (2).
@export Must I be given a copy of a health status report about me (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 7 (3))? Yes, as soon as practicable, if the person obtained your consent to ask for, give or obtain the report and you ask for a copy; they must also consider any written comments you give and keep them with the report.
GIVEN `they obtained my consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person asking for, giving or obtaining the health status report got your consent to do so under s 7
      `I asked for a copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked them for a copy of the report
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7 (3) -- must I be given a copy of the report` `they obtained my consent` `I asked for a copy` IF
        `they obtained my consent`
    AND `I asked for a copy`

§§ `Schedule 1 -- The privacy principles I meet`

-- Principle 6 cl 4: "An entity must not require a consumer, directly or indirectly, to obtain or grant access to a health
-- record about the consumer unless the entity is required or allowed to make the requirement under -- (a) a law of the
-- Territory; or (b) a law of the Commonwealth; or (c) an order of a court." A flat negative with "unless": the text's
-- no unless the law or order applies. Dictionary: "law of the Territory" does not include this Act or the common law.
@export Can an employer, insurer or anyone else make me get, or hand over, my own health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 6 cl 4)? No, unless an ACT law (other than this Act), a Commonwealth law or a court order requires or allows them to make that requirement.
GIVEN `a law or court order allows the requirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an ACT law (other than this Act and the common law), a Commonwealth law, or a court order requires or allows them to make you get or hand over your health record
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `principle 6 cl 4 -- can I be required to get or hand over my own record` `a law or court order allows the requirement` IF
    `a law or court order allows the requirement`

-- Principle 7 cl 3: where the record keeper "is not willing to amend that record ... in accordance with a request by the
-- consumer" and no decision or recommendation to amend is pending or made under a territory or Commonwealth law, the
-- record keeper "must, if the consumer gives ... a written statement concerning the requested correction or addition,
-- take such steps as are reasonable ... to include the statement in the record". "Where" and "if": the text's no
-- otherwise.
@export If the record keeper will not correct my health record, must it add my statement to it (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 7 cl 3)? Yes: if it is not willing to make the correction or addition you asked for, and no decision or recommendation to amend it is pending or made under a law, it must take reasonable steps to include your written statement about the correction in the record.
GIVEN `I asked for a correction or addition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the record keeper to correct or add to the record
      `the record keeper will not make it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record keeper is not willing to make the correction or addition you asked for
      `a decision to amend is pending or made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a decision or recommendation that the record be amended as you asked is pending, or has been made, under an ACT or Commonwealth law (for example on a complaint)
      `I gave a written statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the record keeper a written statement about the correction or addition you asked for
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `principle 7 cl 3 -- must my statement be added to my record` `I asked for a correction or addition` `the record keeper will not make it` `a decision to amend is pending or made` `I gave a written statement` IF
        `I asked for a correction or addition`
    AND `the record keeper will not make it`
    AND (NOT (`a decision to amend is pending or made`))
    AND `I gave a written statement`

-- Principle 4.1 cl 3 (b): destruction is allowed if not prohibited under any other law and it "happens after -- (i) if
-- the consumer is under 18 years old when the information is collected -- the day the consumer turns 25 years old; or
-- (ii) if the consumer is an adult when the information is collected -- 7 years after the day a service was last
-- provided to the consumer by the record keeper". The earliest day is the day after the day named (fork F3 for a
-- 29 February).
@export From what day may my health record be destroyed because enough time has passed (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 4.1 cl 3 (b))? If you were under 18 when the information was collected, only after the day you turn 25; if you were an adult, only after the day 7 years after the record keeper last provided you a service. Other laws may still forbid it, and a health service provider may keep it longer.
GIVEN `I was under 18 when it was collected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the information in the record was collected
      `born on` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `last service on` IS A DATE @desc The last day the record keeper provided a service to you
GIVETH A DATE
`principle 4.1 -- the first day my record may be destroyed for age or time` `I was under 18 when it was collected` `born on` `last service on` MEANS
    IF `I was under 18 when it was collected`
    THEN (`born on` `plus years` 25) `plus days` 1
    ELSE (`last service on` `plus years` 7) `plus days` 1

DECLARE `May my record be destroyed` IS ONE OF
    `no (the text's no): the record keeper must keep it and must not destroy it, even if it is inaccurate (principle 4.1 cl 2)`
    `yes: a territory law requires or allows its destruction (principle 4.1 cl 3 (a))`
    `yes: the age or time in principle 4.1 cl 3 (b) has passed and no other law prohibits destruction (it must then go on the register of destroyed records, principle 4.2)`
    `yes: an accessible electronic copy has been made as the Electronic Transactions Act 2001 s 11 (2) (b) describes (principle 4.1 cl 3 (c))`
    `yes: it is surveillance footage from a security camera at or around the premises (principle 4.1 cl 3 (d))`

-- Principle 4.1 cl 2: "A record keeper must keep, and must not destroy, a health record about a consumer, even if it is
-- later found or claimed to be inaccurate." cl 3: "However, clause 2 does not apply to the destruction ... if" (a)-(d).
-- The flat negative is the text's no unless an exception holds. Principle 4.3 cl 1: a health service provider may keep
-- it longer.
@export May my health record be destroyed yet (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 4.1 cl 2, 3)? The record keeper must keep it, even if it is inaccurate, unless a territory law requires or allows destruction; or no other law forbids it and you were under 18 when it was collected and have turned 25, or 7 years have passed since your last service; or an accessible electronic copy has been made; or it is security-camera footage.
GIVEN `a territory law requires or allows it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an ACT law (other than this Act) requires or allows the record to be destroyed
      `another law prohibits it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any other law prohibits destroying it (for example a court order, or a law requiring records to be kept for a proceeding)
      `I was under 18 when it was collected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 when the information in the record was collected
      `born on` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `last service on` IS A DATE @desc The last day the record keeper provided a service to you
      `an electronic copy was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an electronic copy has been made by a method that reliably keeps the information (Electronic Transactions Act 2001 s 11 (2) (b)) and it will be readily accessible for later reference
      `it is surveillance footage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the record is a video from an automated security or surveillance camera at or around the provider's premises (not a clinical recording)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (or the day the record would be destroyed)
GIVETH A `May my record be destroyed`
`principle 4.1 -- may my record be destroyed` `a territory law requires or allows it` `another law prohibits it` `I was under 18 when it was collected` `born on` `last service on` `an electronic copy was made` `it is surveillance footage` `today` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a territory law requires or allows it`
        THEN `yes: a territory law requires or allows its destruction (principle 4.1 cl 3 (a))`
        IF (NOT (`another law prohibits it`)) AND (NOT (`today` `falls before` (`principle 4.1 -- the first day my record may be destroyed for age or time` `I was under 18 when it was collected` `born on` `last service on`)))
        THEN `yes: the age or time in principle 4.1 cl 3 (b) has passed and no other law prohibits destruction (it must then go on the register of destroyed records, principle 4.2)`
        IF `an electronic copy was made`
        THEN `yes: an accessible electronic copy has been made as the Electronic Transactions Act 2001 s 11 (2) (b) describes (principle 4.1 cl 3 (c))`
        IF `it is surveillance footage`
        THEN `yes: it is surveillance footage from a security camera at or around the premises (principle 4.1 cl 3 (d))`
        OTHERWISE `no (the text's no): the record keeper must keep it and must not destroy it, even if it is inaccurate (principle 4.1 cl 2)`

§§ `Schedule 1 -- When my provider's practice moves or closes (principle 11) -- only if I am told it is moving or closing`

-- Principle 11 cl 2: "Not later than 30 days before the proposed relocation or closure, the provider must -- (a) give
-- public notice ...; and (b) take other practicable steps to inform each consumer who has attended". The last day is the
-- day 30 days before.
@export By when must my health service provider tell me its practice is moving or closing (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 11 cl 2)? Not later than 30 days before the proposed relocation or closure: by public notice, and other practicable steps to inform each consumer who has attended.
GIVEN `the move or closure is on` IS A DATE @desc The day the practice is proposed to relocate (move premises, or stop providing services at that place) or close permanently
GIVETH A DATE
`principle 11 -- by when must I be told the practice is moving or closing` `the move or closure is on` MEANS
    `the move or closure is on` `plus days` (0 MINUS 30)

-- Principle 11 cl 3 (b): the transfer request "must be made not later than 14 days after the day the transfer notice ...
-- is published".
@export By when must I ask for a copy of my health record when my provider's practice moves or closes (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 11 cl 3 (b), 7)? Not later than 14 days after the day the transfer notice is published. If you do not ask, a copy goes to the provider or record keeper named in the notice within 30 days after that period ends.
GIVEN `the notice was published on` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider published the transfer notice
GIVETH A DATE
`principle 11 -- by when must I make my transfer request` `the notice was published on` MEANS
    `the notice was published on` `plus days` 14

-- Principle 11 cl 5: on a transfer request, the copy or written summary must be given "as soon as practicable but not
-- later than the later of -- (a) if a fee is payable for this principle -- 7 days after the day the fee is paid; and
-- (b) 30 days after the day the provider receives the transfer request". cl 6: if the consumer is receiving or needs
-- urgent health services, not later than 7 days after the request is received. A request after the 14-day period is not
-- a transfer request under cl 3 (b) (the text's no). A fee payable and not paid: no day is set yet.
@export By when must I (or the provider I nominate) get the copy of my health record I asked for when my provider's practice moves or closes (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principle 11 cl 5, 6)? As soon as practicable, and not later than 30 days after the provider receives your request, or 7 days after you pay any fee if that is later; if you are receiving or need urgent health services, not later than 7 days after your request. The request must be made within 14 days after the transfer notice is published.
GIVEN `the notice was published on` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider published the transfer notice
      `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider received your transfer request
      `urgent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are receiving or need urgent health services (another provider's advice, even oral, is enough)
      `a fee is payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a fee is payable for the transfer (the notice says so if the Minister has determined one)
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the fee
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`principle 11 -- by when must I get my record` `the notice was published on` `received on` `urgent` `a fee is payable` `the fee was paid` `paid on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (`principle 11 -- by when must I make my transfer request` `the notice was published on`) `falls before` `received on`
        THEN `no duty on these facts (the text's no)`
        IF `urgent`
        THEN `due by` (`received on` `plus days` 7)
        IF `a fee is payable` AND (NOT (`the fee was paid`))
        THEN `no deadline yet: the fee stated in the notice has not been paid`
        IF `a fee is payable`
        THEN `due by` (`the later of` (`paid on` `plus days` 7) `and` (`received on` `plus days` 30))
        OTHERWISE `due by` (`received on` `plus days` 30)

§§ `Schedule 1 -- Moving to a new provider (principles 12.1, 12.2) -- only if I change provider, or my provider changes practice`

-- Principles 12.1 and 12.2 have the same clauses 2-4 (12.1: the consumer moves to a second provider; 12.2: the provider
-- moves practice and the consumer follows). cl 2: (a) if a fee is determined, notice of the fee not later than 7 days
-- after the request; or (b) the copy or summary not later than 30 days after the request. cl 3: the fee paid, the later
-- of 7 days after payment and 30 days after the request. cl 4: urgent health services, not later than 7 days after the
-- request. Fork F10: a fee notice given after the 7 days does not displace cl 2 (b). Fee notice in time and fee not
-- paid: no day is set yet.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE, `urgent` IS A BOOLEAN, `a fee notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN, `fee notice given on` IS A DATE,
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN, `paid on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`the principle 12 day` `received on` `urgent` `a fee notice was given` `fee notice given on` `the fee was paid` `paid on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `urgent`
        THEN `due by` (`received on` `plus days` 7)
        IF `fee notice in time` AND (NOT (`the fee was paid`))
        THEN `no deadline yet: the fee stated in the notice has not been paid`
        IF `fee notice in time`
        THEN `due by` (`the later of` (`paid on` `plus days` 7) `and` (`received on` `plus days` 30))
        OTHERWISE `due by` (`received on` `plus days` 30)
    WHERE
        `fee notice in time` MEANS `a fee notice was given` AND (NOT ((`received on` `plus days` 7) `falls before` `fee notice given on`))

@export By when must my old provider (or old practice) send a copy or summary of my health record to my new provider (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principles 12.1, 12.2)? Not later than 30 days after it receives your request; if it gives you notice of a fee within 7 days, the later of 7 days after you pay and 30 days after the request; if you are receiving or need urgent health services, not later than 7 days after the request.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day your old provider (or the practice your provider left) received your request to send the record to your new provider
      `urgent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are receiving or need urgent health services (another provider's advice, even oral, is enough)
      `a fee notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it gave you notice that you must pay a stated fee before it sends the record
      `fee notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day it gave you that notice (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the fee
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Deadline`
`principle 12 -- by when must my record reach my new provider` `received on` `urgent` `a fee notice was given` `fee notice given on` `the fee was paid` `paid on` MEANS
    `the principle 12 day` `received on` `urgent` `a fee notice was given` `fee notice given on` `the fee was paid` `paid on`

DECLARE `Was my record sent in time` IS ONE OF
    `sent in time`
    `not yet sent, still in time`
    `late: not sent by the day principle 12.1 or 12.2 sets (a breach of the privacy principles you may complain about, s 18 (1) (a))`
    `no day is set yet: the fee has not been paid`

@export Has my old provider sent my health record to my new provider in time (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) schedule 1, principles 12.1, 12.2)? The copy or summary must reach the new provider by the day the principles set (30 days, or 7 days if urgent, or later after a fee); late sending breaches the privacy principles, which you may complain about to the Human Rights Commission.
GIVEN `received on` IS A DATE @desc The day your old provider (or old practice) received your request
      `urgent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are receiving or need urgent health services
      `a fee notice was given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it gave you notice that you must pay a stated fee before it sends the record
      `fee notice given on` IS A DATE @desc The day it gave you that notice (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `the fee was paid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the fee
      `paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day you paid the fee (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `sent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the copy or summary has been given to your new provider
      `sent on` IS A DATE @desc The day it was given (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Was my record sent in time`
`principle 12 -- was my record sent in time` `received on` `urgent` `a fee notice was given` `fee notice given on` `the fee was paid` `paid on` `sent` `sent on` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the principle 12 day` `received on` `urgent` `a fee notice was given` `fee notice given on` `the fee was paid` `paid on`
    WHEN `due by` d THEN
        CONSIDER `the timing under` (`principle 12 -- send the record to the new provider in time` (`the days from` `received on` `to` d)) `the first provider` `give the copy or summary to the new provider` `sent` `sent on` `today` `received on` d
        WHEN `done in time` THEN `sent in time`
        WHEN `not yet done, still in time` THEN `not yet sent, still in time`
        WHEN `out of time` THEN `late: not sent by the day principle 12.1 or 12.2 sets (a breach of the privacy principles you may complain about, s 18 (1) (a))`
    OTHERWISE `no day is set yet: the fee has not been paid`

§§ `Complaints, the court and offences (ss 18, 20-23, 31, 32)`

-- s 18 (1): "A person may make a complaint about an act or omission to the commission on the following grounds: (a) the
-- act or omission contravenes the privacy principles in relation to a consumer; (b) ... a refusal to give access in
-- accordance with this Act ...; (c) ... a refusal by a record keeper ... to give access". (2): about a dead consumer,
-- whether the act happened before or after death. The list is not closed with "only", and other complaints lie under
-- the Human Rights Commission Act 2005 (not deposited): NOTHING outside the grounds.
@export Can I complain to the ACT Human Rights Commission about how my health information or record was handled (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 18)? Yes, about an act or omission that breaches the privacy principles, or that refuses access to a health record (including where access is taken to have been refused); for someone who has died, whether it happened before or after the death. How and when to complain is in the Human Rights Commission Act 2005.
GIVEN `it breaches a privacy principle` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or omission breaches one of the privacy principles in schedule 1 (collection, security and keeping, access by others, correction, use, disclosure, practice closure, transfer to a new provider)
      `it is a refusal of access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a refusal to give you access to a health record under the Act, or the record keeper is taken to have refused (for example, no answer within 2 weeks)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 18 -- can I complain to the commission` `it breaches a privacy principle` `it is a refusal of access` MEANS
    IF `it breaches a privacy principle` OR `it is a refusal of access`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What I want from the Magistrates Court` IS ONE OF
    `a declaration of someone's status under the Act (legally incompetent, guardian, child or young person, a young person's maturity, parental responsibility, legal representative)`
    `a declaration that something done under the Act was or was not validly done`
    `another order about a matter arising under the Act`
    `something else I want`

-- s 31 (1), (2): an application may be made for the declarations or orders in (1) by the health services commissioner
-- or "any person who has a sufficient interest in the matter". Whether the interest is sufficient is the court's view:
-- asked. A grant limited in its own wording: NOTHING outside it.
@export Can I apply to the Magistrates Court about a matter under the health records Act (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 31)? Yes, for a declaration of someone's status under the Act (such as guardian, legal representative, or a young person's maturity), a declaration that something was or was not validly done, or another order about a matter arising under the Act, if you have a sufficient interest in the matter.
GIVEN `what I want` IS A `What I want from the Magistrates Court` @desc What you want the court to declare or order
      `I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a sufficient interest in the matter to warrant your applying (for example, it is your record or you act for the consumer)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 31 -- can I apply to the Magistrates Court` `what I want` `I have a sufficient interest` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`what I want` EQUALS `something else I want`)) AND `I have a sufficient interest`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What the Magistrates Court did` IS ONE OF
    `made, varied or revoked a declaration or order under s 31`
    `refused to make, vary or revoke a declaration or order under s 31`
    `something else the court did`

-- s 32 (1): an appeal to the Supreme Court lies as of right from the making, variation or revocation of a s 31
-- declaration or order, or a refusal. (3): "Despite subsection (1), an appeal does not lie (whether as of right or by
-- leave)" for an interim restraining order: a flat negative, the text's no. Outside s 31: NOTHING.
@export Can I appeal to the Supreme Court against the Magistrates Court's decision under the health records Act (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) s 32)? Yes, as of right, against the making, variation or revocation of a declaration or order under s 31, or a refusal to make, vary or revoke one; but never against an interim restraining order or a refusal of one.
GIVEN `what the court did` IS A `What the Magistrates Court did` @desc What the Magistrates Court did under s 31
      `an interim restraining order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order (or the refusal) was about an interim restraining order
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 32 -- can I appeal to the Supreme Court` `what the court did` `an interim restraining order` MEANS
    IF `what the court did` EQUALS `something else the court did`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `an interim restraining order`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

DECLARE `What they did to me` IS ONE OF
    `threatened, intimidated or recklessly misled me to make me consent, or to act without a needed consent (s 20 (1), (2))`
    `falsely told me I was legally required to consent to a health status report (s 20 (4))`
    `destroyed, defaced or damaged my record, or took it out of the ACT, to evade or frustrate the Act (s 21)`
    `threatened, intimidated or recklessly misled someone to get access to my record they were not entitled to (s 22)`
    `threatened, intimidated or recklessly misled me so that I would not make or pursue, or would withdraw, a request for access (s 23)`
    `something else they did`

-- ss 20-23: each offence carries "Maximum penalty: 50 penalty units, imprisonment for 6 months or both". The penalty unit
-- (Legislation Act 2001 s 133, not deposited) is stated in the republication's note at the republication date: $160 for
-- an individual and $810 for a corporation. Fork F11: the figures are taken from that note.
@export What is the most a person can be fined for this conduct towards me or my health record (Health Records (Privacy and Access) Act 1997 (ACT) ss 20-23)? Each of these offences carries a maximum of 50 penalty units, 6 months imprisonment, or both: $8 000 for an individual, $40 500 for a corporation, at the penalty unit values stated in the 16 November 2025 republication.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did to me` @desc What the person did
      `they are a corporation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who did it is a corporation (a company, for example an insurer or a private hospital) rather than an individual
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`ss 20-23 -- the most they can be fined` `what they did` `they are a corporation` MEANS
    IF `what they did` EQUALS `something else they did`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `they are a corporation`
         THEN JUST (50 TIMES 810)
         ELSE JUST (50 TIMES 160)
